1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,848
This is KubeSage, an AI SRE team for Kubernetes.

2
00:00:03,848 --> 00:00:07,010
Here is what is new, in under three minutes.

3
00:00:08,017 --> 00:00:09,319
First, the summary.

4
00:00:09,319 --> 00:00:12,267
KubeSage puts FinOps and SRE in one system.

5
00:00:12,267 --> 00:00:16,722
It plans the savings, scales for the rush, and fixes what breaks.

6
00:00:16,722 --> 00:00:20,492
Small models do the legwork, and these runs cost cents.

7
00:00:20,492 --> 00:00:22,754
Every finding must cite evidence.

8
00:00:22,754 --> 00:00:24,673
Agent fixes are git commits.

9
00:00:24,673 --> 00:00:29,197
Plans and fixes reach the cluster only when a person applies them.

10
00:00:30,199 --> 00:00:33,622
Picture a hypothetical online store on Azure Kubernetes.

11
00:00:33,622 --> 00:00:37,717
Five services share one node at two hundred eighty dollars a month.

12
00:00:37,717 --> 00:00:41,323
Two of them reserve two cores each while using almost none.

13
00:00:41,323 --> 00:00:46,030
Checkout traffic peaks every weekday morning, and sale days are bigger still.

14
00:00:46,030 --> 00:00:49,269
Tonight, a ledger cache ships with too little memory.

15
00:00:50,276 --> 00:00:53,115
Sales are where FinOps and engineering meet.

16
00:00:53,115 --> 00:00:56,923
From thirty days of usage, KubeSage finds the weekday rush.

17
00:00:56,923 --> 00:00:59,633
Its KEDA rule adds pods ten minutes early.

18
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:04,086
A CPU trigger catches surges no schedule predicts, like a flash sale.

19
00:01:04,086 --> 00:01:09,119
For a date like Black Friday, engineers add their own window to the same rule.

20
00:01:09,119 --> 00:01:13,056
KubeSage also sizes the node pool for the peaks it schedules.

21
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:16,583
Finding the waste is cheap by design.

22
00:01:16,583 --> 00:01:21,763
Statistics are free, and healthy workloads stop after one quick triage call.

23
00:01:21,763 --> 00:01:24,695
Only harder cases get more model reasoning.

24
00:01:24,695 --> 00:01:27,353
Guardrails floor requests from history.

25
00:01:27,353 --> 00:01:30,080
This whole analysis cost about one cent.

26
00:01:31,092 --> 00:01:35,415
When an alert fires, four small agents gather evidence in parallel.

27
00:01:35,415 --> 00:01:38,706
Findings that cite no collected signal are dropped.

28
00:01:38,706 --> 00:01:44,771
A larger Thinker reads the rest, plus your team's plain-English rules, to find the root cause.

29
00:01:44,771 --> 00:01:49,932
An Executor writes the fix as a git commit, after policy checks limit each edit.

30
00:01:51,683 --> 00:01:53,800
Here is the simulated cluster.

31
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:56,411
Each glass is the CPU a pod reserves.

32
00:01:56,411 --> 00:02:02,903
Analyze estimates about thirty-four dollars in monthly savings, for one cent of model calls.

33
00:02:03,514 --> 00:02:09,164
Apply right-sizes the services, moves them to the cheaper node, and adds the KEDA scaling rule.

34
00:02:10,564 --> 00:02:11,804
Now a flash sale:

35
00:02:11,804 --> 00:02:14,649
sixty requests a second, replayed fast.

36
00:02:14,649 --> 00:02:19,244
CPU passes its target, and checkout-web scales out to six pods.

37
00:02:19,863 --> 00:02:21,256
Then the bad release:

38
00:02:21,256 --> 00:02:26,033
ledger-cache runs out of memory, and the ContainerOOMKilled alert fires.

39
00:02:26,662 --> 00:02:28,253
The team starts on its own.

40
00:02:28,253 --> 00:02:33,322
The sub-agents report, the Thinker finds the cause, and the chat shows every hand-off.

41
00:02:36,286 --> 00:02:37,693
The fix is a commit:

42
00:02:37,693 --> 00:02:43,391
more memory, a proposal for a memory-optimised node pool, and two security fixes.

43
00:02:43,391 --> 00:02:45,290
Sixteen seconds, two cents.

44
00:02:45,290 --> 00:02:46,486
A person decides.

45
00:02:47,041 --> 00:02:48,212
That is KubeSage:

46
00:02:48,212 --> 00:02:53,311
FinOps and SRE in one system, built for the rush, with a person in charge.
